## Performance Characteristics

**REST**: HTTP/1.1 overhead ~1KB per request, limited to one request per connection. Best for CRUD operations with ~95% cacheability.

**GraphQL**: Single endpoint reduces network overhead, but query complexity can cause N+1 problems. Apollo Engine shows 30-40% payload reduction vs REST.

**gRPC**: HTTP/2 multiplexing enables concurrent streams. Protocol Buffers reduce payload size by 60-80% vs JSON.

## Use Case Scenarios

```typescript
// REST - Public API
GET /api/users/123/posts

// GraphQL - Mobile app with data requirements
query GetUserPosts($userId: ID!) {
  user(id: $userId) {
    name
    posts(first: 10) {
      title
      comments(count: 3)
    }
  }
}

// gRPC - Internal microservice
service UserService {
  rpc GetUser(GetUserRequest) returns (UserResponse);
}
```

## Error Handling & Authentication

- **REST**: HTTP status codes (200, 404, 500) + JWT/OAuth2
- **GraphQL**: Single 200 response with error payload + JWT
- **gRPC**: Status codes (OK, NOT_FOUND, INTERNAL) + SSL/TLS with token-based auth

## Real-World Applications

- Netflix: REST for public APIs, gRPC for internal services
- GitHub: GraphQL v4 API (95% faster than REST v3)
- Uber: gRPC for microservices communication (30% latency reduction)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of CQRS pattern in real-time collaborative apps - crucial for Miro's architecture where immediate UI feedback and data consistency are both required.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidate will explain: Command side receives shape update request, validates permissions, persists to database, emits ShapeUpdated event. Query side maintains read model for fast UI rendering. Event-driven architecture ensures eventual consistency across all connected clients.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Command
class UpdateShapePositionCommand {
  constructor(public shapeId: string, public x: number, public y: number) {}
}

// Command Handler
class ShapeCommandHandler {
  async handle(command: UpdateShapePositionCommand) {
    const shape = await this.repo.findById(command.shapeId);
    shape.updatePosition(command.x, command.y);
    await this.repo.save(shape);

    // Emit event for query side
    this.eventBus.emit(new ShapeUpdated(shape.id, shape.x, shape.y));
  }
}

// Query side optimized for UI
class ShapeReadModel {
  async getShape(shapeId: string) {
    return this.readDb.shapes.find(shapeId);
  }
}
```

